But here you're accepting the opposition's terms of debate. Would you 
really think taking notes was cheating even if the game *didn't* offer 
individual #naming?

Yes, people use individual #naming for various kinds of notes, either 
because they think it's the only legit way to take notes, or because 
they're playing on a server from various machines and don't want to have 
to take their notes around with them. But it's far from obvious that 
that's what individual #naming is for.

Individual #naming is pretty clearly for keeping track of the properties 
of individual items or stacks of items--reminding yourself that that 
sword is cursed (because your dog stepped on it reluctantly) or that 
those daggers are enchanted (but not being fully ID'd, you don't see the 
enchantment).

My guess as to why individual #naming survives mindwipe is that it's 
envisioned as a physical mark to the item itself, which you find out if 
you try individually #naming an ordinary war hammer "Mjollnir".

Of course, the DevTeam has left in (and purposely put in) lots of 
features that have uses beyond the obvious (and probably original) 
intentions. So there's nothing wrong with using individual #naming in 
different ways. But to my mind, the issue of whether certain types of 
note-taking are or aren't appropriate is completely independent of 
whether there happens to be an in-game feature that duplicates their effect.

